---
import PayrollChart from "../../../components/PayrollChart.astro";
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../../styles/story.css";
import ReadingModes from "../../../components/ReadingModes.astro";
import Cite from "../../../components/Cite.astro";
import SourcedBlock from "../../../components/SourcedBlock.astro";
import StorySources from "../../../components/StorySources.astro";
import StateEvent from "../../../components/StateEvent.astro";
import StoryState from "../../../components/StoryState.astro";
import { readPublishedState, eventPath } from "../../../lib/state.mjs";
import { event, revisionHistory } from "../../../data/jobs202607.mjs";
const state = readPublishedState().events.get("event-jobs-july-2026");
if (!state) throw new Error("Missing publication selection: event-jobs-july-2026");
if (state.status === "merged") return Astro.redirect(eventPath(state.survivor), 301);

---
{state.status === "retired" ? <StateEvent view={state} /> : <BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/jobs/">The job market</a><span>Jobs report &middot; July 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">



      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The jobs report &middot; July 2026</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}</p>
      <p class="railed rail-note" data-layer="fact"><strong>Revised, September 4, 2026.</strong> The figures on this page are the July report as first published on August 7. In its September 4 release the agency revised July: "the change for July was revised up by 44,000, from -23,000 to +21,000," and June from 20,000 to 31,000. On today's series the twelve months to July hold four negative months, not five, and add 371,000 jobs; the seven-month revision total is minus 56,000, not minus 111,000. July gets a second revision on October 2. The <a href="/events/jobs/august-2026/">August story</a> carries the current figures.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-08-revision" passage="the change for July was revised up by 44,000, from -23,000 to +21,000" /></p>
      <ReadingModes />

      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a></li>
          <li><a href="#outlets">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</a></li>
          <li><a href="#claim-check">Claim checked against the record</a></li>
          <li><a href="#markets">What the markets said</a></li>
          <li><a href="#benchmark">The August 28 benchmark check</a></li>
          <li><a href="#unknowns">What we do not know yet</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
      </details></nav>
      <div class="kpi-strip" data-layer="fact">
        {event.kpis.map((k) => (
          <div class="kpi">
            <span class="kpi-value">{k.value}<small>{k.unit ? ` ${k.unit}` : ""}</small></span>
            <span class="kpi-label">{k.label}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <aside class="three-things" data-layer="fact">
        <p class="tt-label">Three things to know</p>
        <ol>
          <li>The first estimate showed the economy losing 23,000 jobs in July, a change the agency itself said "changed little." Forecasters surveyed by Dow Jones had expected a <em>gain</em> of 83,000.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-07" passage="changed little in July" /></li>
          <li>The bad news reaches backward: May was revised down from +129,000 to +63,000 and June from +57,000 to +20,000. Three months that once looked solid now read +63,000, +20,000, and minus 23,000.</li>
          <li>The unemployment rate fell to 4.1 percent, but for an unwelcome reason: the share of adults working or looking for work dropped to 61.4 percent, down 0.7 points since January.</li>
        </ol>
      </aside>
      <p class="railed rail-note" data-layer="fact">Story updated {event.updated}.</p>



      <!-- Proof: event record context, only visible in work mode -->
      <div data-layer="proof" class="event-record-context">
        <p class="sources-line">This story rests on 3 records.</p>
        <ul class="src-list">
          <li><a href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-07/">The Employment Situation, July 2026</a> &middot; Bureau of Labor Statistics</li>
          <li><a href="/records/bls-prebmk-2026/">Preliminary benchmark revision</a> &middot; Bureau of Labor Statistics</li>
          <li><a href="/records/bls-realer-2026-07/">Real Earnings, July 2026</a> &middot; Bureau of Labor Statistics</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- ═══ WHAT HAPPENED ═══ -->
      <section id="what-happened">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The report</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>

        <SourcedBlock source="bls-empsit-2026-07" kind="record">
          <p>The Bureau of Labor Statistics reported on August 7 that total nonfarm payroll employment fell by 23,000 in July and the unemployment rate was 4.1&nbsp;percent.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-07" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">In the agency's own words, both &ldquo;changed little.&rdquo; The declines were concentrated: local government education lost 50,000 jobs &ldquo;after showing little net change over the prior 12 months,&rdquo; and retail trade lost 19,000. One caution before reading that 50,000 literally: these figures are seasonally adjusted. School employment always swings enormously in summer, and the adjustment is designed to remove that swing. The number does not mean 50,000 ordinary summer layoffs; it means school employment came in roughly 50,000 below the seasonal pattern the agency expected for July. Health care added 22,000, below its 12-month average of 36,000.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-07" passage="local government education lost 50,000" /></p>

        <details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>the record</summary>
          <p>Release: &ldquo;Both nonfarm payroll employment (-23,000) and the unemployment rate (4.1 percent) changed little in July&hellip; Employment in local government education declined by 50,000 in July, after showing little net change over the prior 12 months. Retail trade lost 19,000 jobs in July&hellip; In July, employment in health care continued its upward trend (+22,000) but at a slower pace than the average monthly gain over the prior 12 months (+36,000).&rdquo; <a href="/records/bls-empsit-2026-07/" rel="noreferrer">The Employment Situation, July 2026</a>.</p>
        </details>

        <SourcedBlock source="bls-empsit-2026-07" kind="record">
          <p>May's gain was revised down by 66,000, from +129,000 to +63,000. June's was revised down by 37,000, from +57,000 to +20,000. Combined: 103,000 jobs previously reported are now gone from the record.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-07" passage="The change in total nonfarm payroll employment for May was revised down by 66,000, from +129,000 to +63,000, and the change for June was revised down by 37,000, from +57,000 to +20,000." /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="bls-empsit-2026-07" kind="record">
          <p>Average hourly earnings rose 2 cents to $37.62; over the year they are up 3.2&nbsp;percent.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-07" passage="In July, average hourly earnings for all employees on private nonfarm payrolls, at $37.62, were little changed (+2 cents)." /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The composition of the loss matters: private employers added 30,000 jobs in July while government shed 53,000, so the headline decline is a public-sector story, led by the 50,000-job drop in local government education. The report landed nine days after the Federal Reserve voted 9&ndash;3 to hold interest rates, with three regional Fed presidents formally asking for a quarter-point <em>hike</em>, covered in&nbsp;<a href="/events/fed-rate/july-2026/">our July FOMC story</a>.<Cite s="outlet-38a539f4a7084988" passage="30,000" /><Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-07" passage="Government employment" /><Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-07-29" passage="Voting against the monetary policy action" /></p>
      </section>

      <!-- ═══ WHERE THIS SITS ═══ -->
      <section id="where-this-sits">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">History</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Where this sits</h2>

        <SourcedBlock source="fred-payems-2026-07" kind="computed">
          <p>July was the fifth negative month in the past twelve. Over those twelve months the economy added a net 316,000 jobs, about 26,000 a month.<Cite s="fred-payems-2026-07" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">That is a near-stall by the standard of any recent expansion, and choppy rather than steadily weak, with swings from minus 156,000 to plus 214,000 in a single spring.<Cite s="fred-payems-2026-07" /></p>

        <PayrollChart {...event.visual} />

        <SourcedBlock source="bls-empsit-2026-07" kind="record">
          <p>The unemployment rate dipped from 4.2 to 4.1 percent. The labor force shrank by 264,000 people while household employment fell by 87,000. The rate fell because the labor force contracted by three times more than employment did, not because more people found work.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-07" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The jobs report is really two surveys: a survey of employers, which produced the payroll numbers above, and a survey of households, which produces the unemployment rate. Both moved in the same weak direction.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-07" passage="household survey" /></p>

        <aside class="two-surveys" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">Two surveys, same weak direction (July, seasonally adjusted)</p>
          <ul>
            <li><b>&minus;23,000</b> payroll jobs <small>(employer survey)</small></li>
            <li><b>&minus;87,000</b> people employed <small>(household survey)</small></li>
            <li><b>&minus;264,000</b> in the labor force</li>
            <li><b>&minus;178,000</b> unemployed</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>

        <SourcedBlock source="fred-primeage-2026-07" kind="official">
          <p>Participation among workers 25 to 54 rose to 83.4 percent in July from 83.3 in June. It remains below 84.0 in January.<Cite s="fred-primeage-2026-07" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The exit is not uniform across ages. The July decline in overall participation came from outside the prime working ages; the spring slide touched prime-age workers too.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-07" /><Cite s="fred-primeage-2026-07" passage="2026-07-01,83.4" /></p>
      </section>

      <!-- ═══ WHO FEELS IT ═══ -->
      <section id="who-feels-it">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The people in the numbers</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Who feels it</h2>

        <SourcedBlock source="bls-realer-2026-07" kind="record">
          <p>Average hourly earnings rose 2 cents to $37.62, up 3.2&nbsp;percent over the year. Adjusted for inflation, real hourly earnings fell 0.1&nbsp;percent in July and are down 0.2&nbsp;percent over the year.<Cite s="bls-realer-2026-07" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Wages are rising about 3.2&nbsp;percent a year while prices rise faster. Average hourly pay bought slightly less than a year earlier; average weekly pay, which includes hours worked, bought slightly more, up 0.1 percent. Neither average says what happened to the typical worker.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-07" passage="3.2 percent" /><Cite s="bls-realer-2026-07" passage="decreased 0.1 percent" /><Cite s="bls-realer-2026-07" passage="increase in real average weekly earnings" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="bls-empsit-2026-07" kind="record">
          <p>People on temporary layoff rose by 153,000 to 921,000. The long-term unemployed (27 weeks or more) make up 25.5&nbsp;percent of all unemployed people, one in four. People working part time because they could not find full-time work held at 4.8&nbsp;million. People who want work but have stopped looking rose from 1.76 to 1.81&nbsp;million.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-07" passage="Among the unemployed, the number of people on temporary layoff increased by 153,000 to 921,000 in July." /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="fred-u6rate-2026-07" kind="official">
          <p>U-6, the broadest underutilization measure (adds those marginally attached workers and the involuntary part-timers to the officially unemployed), held at 7.9&nbsp;percent, unchanged from June. The headline unemployment rate improved; the broadest measure did not.<Cite s="fred-u6rate-2026-07" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Put together, the shape of July is a hiring stall more than a burst of layoffs: employment growth has stopped and people are leaving the measured labor force, but neither the unemployment rate, the broad measure, nor the count of permanent job losers shows a sudden broad surge in joblessness. The 153,000 rise in temporary layoffs is the one caution in that picture.<Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-07" passage="153,000" /><Cite s="fred-u6rate-2026-07" passage="2026-07-01,7.9" /></p>
      </section>

      <!-- ═══ OUTLETS ═══ -->
      <section id="outlets">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The coverage</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Three outlets' coverage of this report is in our records: CNBC's and CBS's stories from release day, and NBC's morning-of preview, captured before the number came out. Checked against the release:<Cite s="outlet-38a539f4a7084988" /><Cite s="outlet-85fbbbfbda7781f3" /><Cite s="outlet-7708182891f7251f" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="outlet-38a539f4a7084988" kind="record">
          <p><strong>CNBC</strong> reported minus 23,000, the 4.1 percent rate, the May and June revisions, retail's loss of 19,000, health care's below-trend 22,000, and the 2-cent earnings gain. All match the release exactly.<Cite s="outlet-38a539f4a7084988" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Its explanation of the falling unemployment rate (fewer people working or looking) matches the release's participation numbers. CNBC's sector figures (40,000 lost in leisure and hospitality, 53,000 lost in government, 30,000 gain in private payrolls) all match the release. What separates the two documents is emphasis, not arithmetic: the release classes leisure and hospitality among industries whose employment &ldquo;showed little change,&rdquo; while CNBC headlined the sector's drop and attached a cause, &ldquo;a possible consequence of the World Cup tournament ending,&rdquo; that appears nowhere in the release. The number is the agency's; the explanation is the outlet's speculation.<Cite s="outlet-38a539f4a7084988" passage="World Cup" /><Cite s="bls-empsit-2026-07" passage="showed little change" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="outlet-85fbbbfbda7781f3" kind="record">
          <p><strong>CBS</strong> reported the 23,000 loss, the 50,000 and 19,000 sector losses, health care's 22,000, the combined 103,000 revision, and the participation explanation for the falling unemployment rate. All match the release. It adds a second forecast: economists polled by FactSet expected a gain of 95,000.<Cite s="outlet-85fbbbfbda7781f3" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="outlet-7708182891f7251f" kind="record">
          <p><strong>NBC's</strong> morning-of preview expected hiring to pick up to 83,000 and unemployment to hold at 4.2 percent. Both were wrong by mid-morning.<Cite s="outlet-7708182891f7251f" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The preview also fixes the backdrop the report landed in: gasoline at $4.06 a gallon, up 36 percent since late February amid the war with Iran, and inflation at 3.5 percent against the Federal Reserve's 2 percent target.<Cite s="outlet-7708182891f7251f" passage="4.06" /></p>
      </section>

      <!-- ═══ CLAIM CHECK ═══ -->
      <section id="claim-check">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">One claim, examined</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Claim checked against the record</h2>

        <SourcedBlock source="fred-payems-2026-07" kind="computed">
          <p><strong>The claim:</strong> &ldquo;The 12-month average [payroll gain is] down to just 34,000&rdquo; (CNBC, August 7). <strong>The record today:</strong> recomputing from the current published series gives about 26,000 a month over the same window.<Cite s="fred-payems-2026-07" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Neither number is an error: CNBC's was correct on the data available August 7, and later revisions pulled the average lower still.<Cite s="outlet-38a539f4a7084988" passage="34,000" /><Cite s="alfred-payems-vintages-2026" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">An earlier version of this page read that aging as proof that &ldquo;this year's first estimates have repeatedly overstated the job market.&rdquo; The full first-print record says something more specific. Three months were revised down, three up, one is unchanged so far. The overstatement is real but concentrated: May and June were cut by 109,000 and 37,000. The spring months were revised <em>up</em>.<Cite s="alfred-payems-vintages-2026" /></p>

        <div class="rev-table-wrap" data-layer="fact">
          <table class="rev-table">
            <caption>2026 payroll changes: first estimate vs. today's estimate (thousands)</caption>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Month</th><th scope="col">First estimate</th><th scope="col">Current</th><th scope="col">Revision</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {revisionHistory.map((r) => (
                <tr><th scope="row">{r.month}</th><td>{r.first > 0 ? `+${r.first}` : r.first}</td><td>{r.current > 0 ? `+${r.current}` : r.current}</td><td class={r.current - r.first < 0 ? "rev-down" : ""}>{r.current - r.first > 0 ? `+${r.current - r.first}` : r.current - r.first === 0 ? "0" : r.current - r.first}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p class="rev-note" data-layer="proof">Across the seven months: average revision &minus;16,000; cumulative &minus;111,000; average miss in either direction 44,000. First estimates from the ALFRED vintage archive; current estimates from the July release of August 7. The revision note at the top of the page carries the September 4 figures.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ═══ MARKETS ═══ -->
      <section id="markets">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The reaction</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What the markets said</h2>

        <SourcedBlock source="outlet-38a539f4a7084988" kind="record">
          <p>Right after the Fed's July 29 decision, NBC News quoted CME FedWatch odds of a September rate hike at 59 percent. After this report, CNBC quoted them at 44 percent. Stock futures rose; Treasury yields fell.<Cite s="outlet-1dccf05cb4b25801" passage="59%" /><Cite s="outlet-38a539f4a7084988" passage="44%" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The move, not the level, is the story. On July 29 the Fed had voted 9&ndash;3 to hold, with three members formally asking for a quarter-point hike. Between the Fed's decision and this report, nine days apart, a rate increase went from more likely than not to less; this report is the main event in that window, not the only one, and markets treated the move as good news.<Cite s="fomc-statement-2026-07-29" passage="preferred to raise the target range for the federal funds rate by 1/4 percentage point" /><Cite s="outlet-1dccf05cb4b25801" passage="59%" /><Cite s="outlet-38a539f4a7084988" passage="44%" /></p>
      </section>

      <!-- ═══ BENCHMARK ═══ -->
      <section id="benchmark">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Since this page was written</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">The August 28 benchmark check</h2>

        <SourcedBlock source="bls-prebmk-2026" kind="record">
          <p>Once a year the agency checks its survey-based payroll count against near-complete counts from state unemployment-insurance records. For March 2026: the survey was 79,000 jobs too high (0.1&nbsp;percent), with private payrolls 178,000 too high. The average size of this correction over the past ten years is 0.2 percent.<Cite s="bls-prebmk-2026" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <p data-layer="narrative">This correction does not change the figures on this page yet: it is applied to the published series in February 2027. Private payrolls 178,000 too high and the total only 79,000 too high means government payrolls were counted about 99,000 too <em>low</em> as of March. The check covers one month, March, and does not say which months the error sits in, so it neither confirms nor complicates July's school figures.<Cite s="bls-prebmk-2026" passage="-178,000" /></p>

        <details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>the record</summary>
          <p>Release: &ldquo;The preliminary estimate of the CES national benchmark revision to total nonfarm employment for March 2026 was -79,000 (-0.1 percent)&hellip; The preliminary benchmark revision for total private employment was -178,000 (-0.1 percent).&rdquo; <a href="/records/bls-prebmk-2026/" rel="noreferrer">Preliminary Benchmark (National), March 2026</a>, August 28, 2026.</p>
        </details>
      </section>

      <!-- ═══ UNKNOWNS ═══ -->
      <section id="unknowns">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Open questions</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>
        <ul data-layer="fact">
          <li>Why local government education fell by 50,000 in one month. The release states the figure without a cause, and we will not guess at one.</li>
          <li>Which months created the benchmark gap. The month-by-month distribution arrives with the final revision in February 2027.</li>
          <li>Whether the labor-force exit continues. One month of a shrinking labor force alongside flat broad underutilization reads as a hiring stall; a second month would start to read as something worse.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- ═══ RECORDS (proof-layer, visible in work mode) ═══ -->
      <StorySources event="jobs/july-2026" />
      <StoryState view={state} supporting />
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>}

<style is:global>
  /* ── Sourced block: binds paragraph + chip + receipt ── */
  .sourced-block { margin: 1rem 0; }
  .sourced-block > p { margin: 0; }
  .sourced-block > .chip { margin: 0.15rem 0 0; }

  /* In story mode, chips inside sourced-blocks are hidden */
  /* Numbered source citations: visible in every mode */
  .src-ref { font-size: 0.72em; line-height: 0; margin-left: 0.15rem; }
  .src-ref a { color: var(--muted, #6b7280); text-decoration: none; }
  .src-ref a:hover { color: var(--ink); text-decoration: underline; }
  .src-list li { scroll-margin-top: 4rem; }

  /* Layer visibility is per-ELEMENT (CSS attribute selectors), not inherited like
     the gate's ancestor-coverage check: a proof span inside a fact container is
     still hidden in non-work modes. That asymmetry is intentional. */
  .sourced-block > .chip { display: none; }
  .mode-work .sourced-block > .chip { display: inline-block; }

  /* In work mode, sourced blocks get a subtle left border showing source binding */
  .mode-work .sourced-block {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--blue);
    padding-left: 1rem;
  }
  .mode-work .sourced-block[data-source*="fred"],
  .mode-work .sourced-block[data-source*="computed"] {
    border-left-color: var(--ink);
  }
  .mode-work .sourced-block[data-source*="outlet"] {
    border-left-color: var(--amber);
  }

  /* ── Figcaption provenance: proof-only ── */
  .fig-provenance {
    display: none;
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--slate);
  }
  .mode-work .fig-provenance { display: block; }

  .event-record-context { margin: 0 0 1.5rem; }
</style>
